% ============================================================================
% fungus-parts — the structural parts of a fungus and the defining token /
% role the source states for each one (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A mushroom is the part of a fungus we see, but the FUNGUS is an assembly of
% named parts, and a mycology key names each part by the ONE thing it is or
% does. "A hypha is a thread; the mycelium is the body made of hyphae; the
% fruiting body carries the spores; the cap is the apical surface; a spore
% disperses the fungus; the gills hold the spores; the stalk lifts the cap."
% Before you can reason about a fungus — identify it, key it out, or teach it —
% you must first know WHICH part you are looking at and what that part is FOR.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? fungus_part_role(mycelium, $Role)      % made_of_hyphae
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `fungus_part_role(part, role)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the role
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these fungus parts (it never invents a role).
%
% Each fungus part and the defining token / role the source states for it, as
% a little truth table:
%
%     fungus part    defining token / role      a beginner's cue
%     -----------    ---------------------      -----------------------------
%     hypha          thread_like                one fine thread of the fungus
%     mycelium       made_of_hyphae             the body, a tangle of hyphae
%     fruiting_body  contains_spores            the mushroom that bears spores
%     cap            apical_surface             the top, the umbrella
%     spore          disperses                  the seed-like dispersal cell
%     gills          holds_spores               blades under the cap
%     stalk          lifts_cap                  the stem that raises the cap
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a role and recall the fungus part:
%
%     ? fungus_part_role($Part, made_of_hyphae)   % mycelium
%
% A word that is NOT one of these fungus parts — `root`, `leaf`, `chloroplast`
% — is deliberately NOT a row, so a fungus-part recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a role. (A fungus has no roots: the mycelium is root-LIKE, but
% it is not a root.) That honest-abstention property is what makes the table
% safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the part→role pairs it can
% ground, and only those.
%
% The `role` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a word
% the source EXPLICITLY uses for that part — never one the source does not
% support. Where the source's phrase is more than one word (the mycelium is
% "made up of many hyphae"; the stalk "lifts the cap"), the atom joins the
% source's own words with underscores, so every value stays literally checkable
% against the quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→role pair below
% was read out of a single fetched UNLV School of Life Sciences teaching page,
% and any pair whose role could not be verified there was dropped). Each pair,
% with the verbatim span that states it, from "The Kingdom Fungi" (Matthew
% Landau, University of Nevada Las Vegas biology faculty teaching page):
%   https://landau.faculty.unlv.edu/fungi.htm
%
%   hypha → thread_like
%     "thread-like structure of very fine, colorless threads, that makes up the
%      body of a fungus"
%
%   mycelium → made_of_hyphae
%     "The body of a fungus, made up of many hyphae, is called a mycelium."
%
%   fruiting_body → contains_spores
%     "it develops one or more fruiting bodies containing reproductive spores."
%
%   cap → apical_surface
%     "cap - apical surface; on edible fungi usu. rounded and smooth."
%
%   spore → disperses
%     "A spore is a tiny, usually haploid, cell that disperses the fungus to new
%      habitats, usually by floating thru the air."
%
%   gills → holds_spores
%     "gills - flat-sided, blade-like radial structures on the underside of the
%      cap, covered with microscopic basidia, which holds the mushroom's spores."
%
%   stalk → lifts_cap
%     "stalk - typically a cylindrical structure that lifts the cap above the
%      soil surface, like a stem or shoot in seed-bearing plant."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the UNLV statement that fixes the
% first row (hypha → thread_like). Every OTHER row's verbatim span is listed
% above, so each role remains independently checkable. The source is a
% university biology faculty teaching page (a .edu teaching summary, not a
% primary reference), so `trust consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table fungus_part_role {
    columns part, role

    row (hypha, thread_like)
    row (mycelium, made_of_hyphae)
    row (fruiting_body, contains_spores)
    row (cap, apical_surface)
    row (spore, disperses)
    row (gills, holds_spores)
    row (stalk, lifts_cap)

    source "thread-like structure of very fine, colorless threads, that makes up the body of a fungus"
    locator "https://landau.faculty.unlv.edu/fungi.htm"
    trust consensus
}
